{
 "duration": 96.12,
 "text": "A mother, a toddler, a baby, and a stolen boat. Escaping across 5,000 kilometres of open sea, 1791. Her name is Mary Bryant, a young highway robber sent from England to the brand new prison colony in Sydney. The colony is starving. Her husband William, another convict, decides they're not staying. So they don't just steal a boat, they steal the governor's boat. One moonless night they slip out of Sydney Harbour, 11 people in one open boat. They sail north up the entire east coast of Australia. Storms nearly swamp them. They squeeze between the Great Barrier Reef and the coast, round the tip of Australia and out across open sea. 69 days later they land on the island of Timor, over 5,000 kilometres. Every single one of them alive. Their cover story? They're survivors of a shipwreck. And the Dutch officials believe it for a while. Then a real shipwreck crew arrives, a British Navy captain who'd been hunting the mutineers of the Bounty. Their story falls apart. They're arrested and shipped back in chains. On the way her baby son dies, then her husband, then her little daughter. Mary reaches London alone, facing a possible death sentence for escaping. But famous writer James Boswell takes up her case. She's pardoned and he sends her money for the If Mary deserves a like for sheer nerve, you know what to do. That's episode one of Impossible Journeys. Follow along for the next one. And it all started with...",
